+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`src/index.ts` already has `applyPatch`, a prototype-safe `clone`, `jsonEqual` (RFC 6902 §4.6) and `decodeToken`. Pointer escaping (the inverse of `decodeToken`) does not exist yet. See proposal.md for motivation.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:** a small recursive diff that satisfies the spec's minimality properties; reuse existing helpers; no dependencies.
+
+**Non-Goals:** globally shortest patches; `copy`/`test` generation.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Objects**: walk own keys (`Object.keys`/`Object.hasOwn`, safe for `__proto__`). Keys only in `from` → `remove`; only in `to` → `add` (cloned value); in both → recurse. Alternative of replacing whole objects was rejected as it violates "only what changed".
+- **Arrays**: strip the common prefix and suffix (by `jsonEqual`), then compute a longest-common-subsequence over the middle. Matched items are untouched; each gap between matches has some removed and some inserted items. Gap items are paired positionally and diffed recursively in place (so a changed item gets a nested patch), surplus old items are `remove`d and surplus new items are `add`ed. Operations are emitted left to right while tracking the current index, so each path is valid at the moment it is applied. LCS guarantees an insert/removal never rewrites neighbouring items and a move becomes a `remove` plus an `add` — which RFC 6902 §4.4 permits and the request accepts. Emitting `move` was not chosen: it adds index bookkeeping for a one-operation saving.
+- **Replace vs recurse**: objects and arrays at the same location are always diffed member by member (never replaced wholesale). Only for items paired positionally inside an array gap, when the nested diff is not smaller (serialized length) than a single `replace`, use `replace`, so unrelated items do not produce many ops. Differing types or scalars → `replace`. Root differing in type → `replace` at `""` (§4.3 allows it).
+- **Aliasing**: values placed in `add`/`replace` are deep-cloned with the existing `clone`.
+- **Escaping**: `~`→`~0` first, then `/`→`~1` (RFC 6901 §3).
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- LCS is O(n·m) in the differing middle of an array → prefix/suffix trimming makes the common case linear; acceptable for the document sizes this library handles.
+- Positional pairing in gaps may produce a nested patch where `remove`+`add` would be smaller → mitigated by the size check on paired items.
