+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+Clients want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document rather than the whole new version. The library can apply a JSON Patch but cannot produce one.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Add an exported `createPatch(from, to)` that returns a JSON Patch (RFC 6902) which, applied to `from` with `applyPatch`, yields a document equal to `to`.
+- The patch is minimal in the sense of the request: unchanged members and items are never mentioned; an array insertion or removal is a single `add` or `remove`; a moved array item is expressed without rewriting the items between its old and new position.
+- Inputs are never mutated and the patch shares no structure with them.
+- Not breaking: purely additive export.
+
+Governing standards: JSON Patch RFC 6902 (§3 patch structure, §4 operations, §4.6 equality) and JSON Pointer RFC 6901 (§3 syntax, §3/§4 escaping of `~` and `/`).
+
+Out of scope: guaranteeing the globally shortest patch (only the properties above), generating `copy` or `test` operations, JSON Merge Patch generation, and non-JSON inputs (`undefined`, functions, cycles).
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+- `json-patch`: adds a requirement for generating a patch from two documents.
+
+## Impact
+
+- `src/index.ts`: new exported function; `test/patch.test.ts` (or a new test file): new tests.
+- No new dependencies; no changes to existing behaviour.
